# runs/g2-nvme2.json (r8gd.16xlarge, as runs/g2-nvme.json): the follow-up to
# results/g2/20261006-204259-8fb808f. That run found that the pristine mmap baseline is bound by
# read-around: md0's read_ahead_kb is 2048, about 1.7 MB is read per major fault, and every
# SRR062634_2000000 rung hit the 1800 s cap. Its TTL left the per-accession baseline, the
# profiles and rep 2 unrun.
# Upstream at its best (Law 2) includes a host tune the user can make without touching the code:
# the block device's read_ahead_kb. These cells are labelled mmap[read_ahead_kb=N]; the binary is
# the pristine upstream build. Cheapest first.
note #21/#23 follow-up: read_ahead_kb host tuning for upstream -M, per-accession baseline, profiles, rep 2 of the madv ladders
readahead 4
run mmap SRR062634_250000-gz cold 1 8
readahead 128
run mmap SRR062634_250000-gz cold 1 8
readahead 4
run mmap SRR062634_2000000-gz cold 1 64 16
run mmap SRR062634_2000000-gz warm 1 64 8
run mmap SRR062634_2000000-fq warm 1 64 8
run mmap SRR062634_200000-gz cold 1 64
run mmap ERR478965_200000-gz cold 1 64
run mmap ERR598966_200000-gz cold 1 64
run mmap SRR28305653_200000-gz cold 1 64
run mmap SRR062634_200000-gz warm 1 64
run mmap ERR478965_200000-gz warm 1 64
run mmap ERR598966_200000-gz warm 1 64
run mmap SRR28305653_200000-gz warm 1 64
profile mmap SRR062634_2000000-gz cold 64
profile mmap SRR062634_2000000-fq warm 64
profile mmap SRR062634_2000000-fq warm 8
readahead default
run madv SRR062634_2000000-gz cold 1 64 48 32 24 16 12 8
run madv SRR062634_250000-gz cold 1 8 4 2 1
run mmap SRR062634_200000-gz cold 1 64
readahead 128
run mmap SRR062634_2000000-gz cold 1 64
